WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.443 --> 00:00:04.923
One question I often get here is whether
the order of articles matters for data

2
00:00:04.923 --> 00:00:05.883
minimisation.

3
00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:15.492
So for this person under 18, we see that
we find out early that they're 17, and so

4
00:00:15.492 --> 00:00:19.172
we don't need to look at the rest of
the machine-executable legislation.

5
00:00:21.744 --> 00:00:25.744
But isn't that because age is one
of the first articles described?

6
00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:32.347
And if it had happened to come later,
wouldn't we have looked at that sensitive

7
00:00:32.347 --> 00:00:32.788
data?

8
00:00:32.788 --> 00:00:34.868
And the answer is yes.

9
00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.948
In this case, certainly.

10
00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:41.750
And we're having discussions
with lawyers about this.

11
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.630
Are you allowed to change
the order of legislation?

12
00:00:45.531 --> 00:00:49.051
So can you choose which
articles you execute first?

13
00:00:52.012 --> 00:00:54.613
Lawyers don't necessarily agree on that.

14
00:00:54.613 --> 00:00:59.093
For some legislation, the order in which
you execute the articles probably doesn't

15
00:00:59.093 --> 00:01:00.235
matter.

16
00:01:00.235 --> 00:01:02.816
And for other legislation it perhaps does.

17
00:01:02.816 --> 00:01:06.736
Maybe a later article assumes
you've already read an earlier one?

18
00:01:08.415 --> 00:01:12.735
Either way, if you have room to choose
the order of execution, you could minimise

19
00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:13.535
this.

20
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:23.422
So you can make sure as few data points as
possible are touched, or as few sensitive

21
00:01:23.422 --> 00:01:24.222
ones.

22
00:01:25.326 --> 00:01:29.886
And maybe we could even go as far as
letting the GDPR determine that order.

23
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:39.833
That law would act as a kind of meta-layer,
saying what is sensitive, what is less

24
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:42.433
so, and what you may or may not touch.
